{"name":"Frontier Land","hex":"#bca59a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"frontier","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The part of a country which borders or faces another country or unsettled region.","The most advanced or recent version of something; the leading edge."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To live as pioneers on frontier territory.","To place on the frontier."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/frontier"},{"term":"land","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The part of Earth which is not covered by oceans or other bodies of water.","Real estate or landed property; a partitioned and measurable area which is owned and acquired and on which buildings and structures can be built and erected."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To descend to a surface, especially from the air.","To alight, to descend from a vehicle."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/land"}],"entries":[{"title":"Frontier","blocks":[{"text":"A frontier is a political and geographical term referring to areas near or beyond a boundary, particularly zones at the edge of settled or governed territory. The concept carries distinct meanings in different national and cultural contexts, but broadly describes a transitional zone between an established civilization and unknown, ungoverned, or contested land. In colonial and post-colonial history, frontiers played a central role in the expansion of European powers into the Americas, Asia, and Australia. Countries such as Brazil, Argentina, Chile, and the United States each developed distinct frontier cultures, mythologies, and policies shaped by encounters with indigenous peoples, geographic conditions, and imperialist ambitions. In the United States, the concept was most influentially theorized by historian Frederick Jackson Turner, whose Frontier Thesis (1893) argued that the frontier experience was the defining force in shaping American democracy and national character. Canada developed a parallel historiographical tradition through scholars such as Harold Adams Innis. Beyond the Americas, frontier zones shaped imperial expansion in Russia, China, and the Indian subcontinent. The word \"frontier\" entered English from French in the 15th century with the meaning \"borderland\"; its use to describe the edge of settled territory is a distinctly North American development."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/1800s_Texas_House.JPG/330px-1800s_Texas_House.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frontier","wikidata":"Q1355821"},{"title":"Land","blocks":[{"text":"Land, also known as dry land, ground, or earth, is the solid terrestrial surface of Earth not submerged by the ocean or another body of water. It makes up approximately 29% of Earth's surface and includes all continents and islands. Earth's land surface is almost entirely covered by regolith, a layer of rock, soil, and minerals that forms the outer part of the crust. Land plays an important role in Earth's climate system, being involved in the carbon cycle, nitrogen cycle, and water cycle. One-third of land is covered in trees another third is used for agriculture, and roughly one-tenth is covered in glacier ice. The remainder consists of desert, savannah, and prairie."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Coastline_as_seen_from_Chimney_Rock%2C_Point_Reyes_National_Seashore.jpg/330px-Coastline_as_seen_from_Chimney_Rock%2C_Point_Reyes_National_Seashore.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Land","wikidata":"Q11081619"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/StateLibQld_1_113072_Bushman_with_his_dog_and_horse_outside_a_humpy%2C_Hughenden_district%5E%2C_1910-1920.jpg/500px-StateLibQld_1_113072_Bushman_with_his_dog_and_horse_outside_a_humpy%2C_Hughenden_district%5E%2C_1910-1920.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/StateLibQld_1_113072_Bushman_with_his_dog_and_horse_outside_a_humpy%2C_Hughenden_district%5E%2C_1910-1920.jpg/500px-StateLibQld_1_113072_Bushman_with_his_dog_and_horse_outside_a_humpy%2C_Hughenden_district%5E%2C_1910-1920.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Australian bushman with his dog and horse, c. 1910","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStateLibQld_1_113072_Bushman_with_his_dog_and_horse_outside_a_humpy%2C_Hughenden_district%5E%2C_1910-1920.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Lampi%C3%A3o_e_Benjamin_Abrah%C3%A3o_Botto.jpg/500px-Lampi%C3%A3o_e_Benjamin_Abrah%C3%A3o_Botto.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Lampi%C3%A3o_e_Benjamin_Abrah%C3%A3o_Botto.jpg/1280px-Lampi%C3%A3o_e_Benjamin_Abrah%C3%A3o_Botto.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Lampi%C3%A3o_e_Benjamin_Abrah%C3%A3o_Botto.jpg/500px-Lampi%C3%A3o_e_Benjamin_Abrah%C3%A3o_Botto.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Lampião's outlaw band, wearing traditional sertanejo attire. The mythology built on the image of cangaceiros defines Brazil's view of the Sertão as a frontier. ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALampi%C3%A3o_e_Benjamin_Abrah%C3%A3o_Botto.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Machado%2C_Fernandes_-_Posse_da_Amaz%C3%B4nia%2C_Acervo_do_Museu_Paulista_da_USP.jpg/500px-Machado%2C_Fernandes_-_Posse_da_Amaz%C3%B4nia%2C_Acervo_do_Museu_Paulista_da_USP.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/